#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# flatten

#% text
# `flatten(xs)` gives the elements of the nested list `xs` at every depth, as one flat list.

#% text
# `flatten(xs, d)` flattens only the first `d` levels of nesting.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Only sublists with the same head as `xs` are spliced in; other terms stay whole.

#% item
# The order is the order in which the elements are written.

#% item
# `flatten(xs, 1)` removes one level: a list of lists becomes the list of their elements.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
flatten([[1, 2], [3, [4, 5]], 6])
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]

#% cell
flatten([[1, [2]], [[3]]], 1)
#% output
#= [1, [2], [3]]

#% cell
flatten([])
#% output
#= []

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Terms with other heads are kept:

#% cell
flatten([f(1, 2), [3, 4]])
#% output
#= [f(1, 2), 3, 4]

#% text
# A matrix as the list of its entries, row by row:

#% cell
flatten([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]

#% text
# Every level:

#% cell
flatten([1, [2, [3, [4]]]], inf)
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3, 4]

#% text
# Only the parts with a given head:

#% cell
flatten(f(g(a, b), f(c)), inf, g)
#% output
#= f(a, b, f(c))

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# All the coordinates of a list of points, for their range:

#% cell
pts = [[0.5, 2.0], [1.5, -1.0], [3.0, 0.25]]
[min(flatten(pts)), max(flatten(pts))]
#% output
#= [-1.0, 3.0]

#% text
# A list of lists of results built in a nested table, made flat:

#% cell
flatten(table(i*j, i, 3, j, i))
#% output
#= [1, 2, 4, 3, 6, 9]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# One level of flattening is `catenate` for a list of lists:

#% cell
flatten([[1, 2], [3]], 1) === catenate([[1, 2], [3]])
#% output
#= true

#% text
# `flat_map` maps and then flattens one level:

#% cell
flat_map(k => [k, -k], [1, 2, 3])
#% output
#= [1, -1, 2, -2, 3, -3]

#% text
# The number of entries of a matrix:

#% cell
length(flatten([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]))
#% output
#= 6

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `catenate`, `join`, `flat_map`, `partition`, `depth`
